20:30:16 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: While electric cars can be cheaper to operate than gasoline cars, the same simple cost advantage doesn’t always apply to home heating. The reasons come down to **fundamental differences in efficiency, energy pricing, and technology** between the two applications. Here’s a breakdown: --- ### 1. The efficiency advantage is not the same - **Cars:** An electric motor converts over 85–90% of the electrical energy into motion. A gasoline engine typically converts only 20–30% of the fuel’s energy into motion—the rest is lost as heat. This massive efficiency gap means electricity can be *more expensive per unit of energy* and still come out cheaper per mile. - **Home heating (electric resistance):** A standard electric resistance heater (baseboard, space heater, electric furnace) is nearly 100% efficient at turning electricity into heat *inside your home*. But a modern natural gas furnace is also 90–98% efficient. So there’s no huge efficiency multiplier—unlike electric motors vs. internal combustion engines. Worse, if the electricity was generated by burning fossil fuels at a power plant (~40% efficient) and then transmitted, the overall “primary energy to heat” chain can be less efficient than burning gas directly. --- ### 2. Cost per unit of useful energy differs - **Cars:** In many regions, the cost to drive a mile on electricity is lower than on gasoline. For example, $0.12/kWh and 3 miles/kWh yields $0.04/mile, while $3.50/gallon and 30 MPG yields ~$0.12/mile. - **Heating:** Compare the cost to produce 1 million BTUs (a standard heating unit): - **Electric resistance:** At $0.12/kWh, that’s about $35 per million BTU (since 1 kWh = 3,412 BTU, and 1 million / 3412 ≈ 293 kWh × $0.12 = $35). - **Natural gas:** At $1.20/therm (1 therm = 100,000 BTU, furnace at 95% efficiency), you need about 10.5 therms for the same heat, costing ~$12.60. In this common scenario, gas heat is roughly **one-third the cost** of electric resistance heat. That’s a huge economic reason why people don’t switch—and why all-new electric resistance homes are rare in gas-served areas. --- ### 3. Heat pumps change the game, but with caveats A modern electric heat pump doesn’t generate heat—it moves it. With a typical coefficient of performance (COP) of 2–4, it can deliver 2–4 times more heat energy than the electrical energy it consumes. This can bring the cost of electric heating down to, or even below, natural gas in many climates and at certain electricity prices. So why not “all houses” use heat pumps? - **Upfront cost:** Installing a heat pump system (especially ground-source) is often more expensive than a gas furnace, even with incentives. - **Climate limits:** Air-source heat pumps lose efficiency in very cold weather. They often require backup (electric resistance or gas), reducing the average COP. - **Infrastructure lock-in:** Many houses already have gas lines and gas furnaces. Switching to an all-electric system can mean expensive retrofits (electrical panel upgrades, new ductwork or mini-splits). - **Electricity prices vary:** In regions where electricity is cheap (e.g., abundant hydro) and natural gas is expensive or unavailable, all-electric heat is common (like Quebec). In most of the U.S. and Europe, gas remains cheaper on a per-BTU basis. --- ### 4. The “apples to oranges” energy markets Gasoline is a globally traded commodity priced per gallon, while electricity pricing includes generation, transmission, distribution, taxes, and fixed charges. Natural gas prices for heating are often much lower per unit energy than the residential electricity rate equivalent. Simply put, burning gas directly in a home is often the cheapest way to produce heat, whereas burning gasoline in an engine is not the cheapest way to move a car. --- ### In short: Electric cars beat gasoline cars because electric motors are **dramatically more efficient** than combustion engines. For heating, electric resistance offers no such leap over a high-efficiency gas furnace, making it much more expensive unless a heat pump is used. Even then, the economic viability of switching all houses to electric heat depends heavily on local energy prices, climate, and installation costs—which is why we still see a mix of heating systems worldwide. There’s no contradiction; it’s two very different slices of the energy pie.
