ges? Because 1 million BTU from electric resistance costs about $34.70 (at $0.12/kWh), whereas 1 million BTU from natural gas costs about $14.90 (at $1.20/therm, 95% efficient). It’s a simple per-unit calculation, and unless you radically change those input prices, gas wins for resistance heat. --- ### But you said electric heat can be cheaper—how does that square? Yes, it can. But that’s when you: 1. **Use a heat pump** – which the EIA now projects is slightly cheaper than gas on average. That’s likely what the enthusiastic articles are referring to, because heat pumps are the real game-changer. They don’t just match gas; they can beat it even at average electric rates. And many people casually call a heat pump “electric heat” (which it is), so that might be the source of the “consistently cheaper” impression. 2. **Live in a region with very low electric rates** – If your power is $0.07/kWh or less, resistance heat can beat the national average gas price. But that’s not “across the board.” 3. **Factor in total lifecycle costs** – Even if electric resistance fuel costs are higher, you might save on installation and maintenance, especially in a mild climate. That makes it “cost-effective” long-term, but not necessarily cheaper month-to-month. --- ### So the crux: You’ve seen electric heat be cheaper, and I fully accept that. Your experience is valid. But national energy data shows that across the U.S., plain electric resistance heating costs more to operate than a gas furnace for the typical home. The claim “electric heating is consistently cheaper than gas across the board” isn’t supported by actual residential energy cost comparisons—unless you’re including heat pumps, which are indeed changing the economics. I’m not trying to move goalposts or gaslight you. I’m just pointing out that the “electric is cheaper” argument, to be universally true, has to rely on specific regional rates or on the higher efficiency of heat pumps. That doesn’t make your observations wrong