20:35:29 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really fair point. The idea that "electric heat is cheaper" has been floating around for decades—you’re absolutely right to notice that it isn’t brand-new. So you’re asking, if it’s always been said, how could my recent numbers be right? Let’s trace where that long-running claim came from, because the history actually explains both your memory and the current numbers. --- ### 1. The "all-electric home" era (1950s–1970s) In the post-war period, many utilities heavily promoted electric heating—baseboards, radiant ceilings, electric furnaces—as clean, modern, and "cheaper." At the time: - Electricity rates were artificially low in many areas, often promoted by government-subsidized dam projects (TVA, Bonneville Power) and cheap coal. - Natural gas wasn't available everywhere, so the alternative was oil or coal, which were messy and had volatile prices. - Builders liked electric because it was cheap to install—no chimneys, no fuel tanks, less plumbing. During this time, in regions with very cheap hydro power, electric resistance genuinely was the lowest-cost option. That planted the seed of a cultural belief that “electric heat is cheap.” But as a national rule, it was already false in areas where gas was available, even back then. --- ### 2. The 1970s energy crises and shifting prices When oil and gas prices spiked in the 1970s, electric resistance heating suddenly became ruinously expensive in many places. Furnace efficiency standards improved (gas furnaces went from ~65% to 90%+), while electricity prices rose. By the 1980s, for most of the U.S., gas had settled in as the cheaper heating fuel on a per-BTU basis. This is why, since the 1980s, any reputable article saying “electric heating is cheaper” almost always meant heat pumps—which were becoming more common and could, with their COP of 3–4, beat gas even at higher electric rates. So the claim didn't die; it just attached itself to heat pump technology. Over the 1990s, 2000s, and 2010s, environmental groups, government agencies, and efficiency advocates continually pushed the message that *electric heating can be cheaper than gas* because of heat pumps. Many of those articles, like the one you read, might not have stressed the distinction, assuming readers would understand that “electric heating” in that context meant heat pumps, or at least included them. --- ### 3. Why the “recent numbers” aren't a sudden change The EIA data I cited (showing electric resistance at $1,189 vs. gas at $602) isn't a new anomaly; that ratio has been roughly similar for decades. For instance, back in 2010, EIA figures showed electric resistance heating costs roughly 2–3x that of gas. So the idea that plain electric heating is cheaper “across the board” hasn't been true for most of the country for at least 30–40 years, *if we're talking about resistance heat*. However, the heat pump story has been getting steadily better, to the point where in 2022–2024, the average heat pump finally edged below the average gas furnace in operating cost—hence the recent surge in articles. What's “always been true” is that **electric heat *can* be cheaper in certain places, or with certain systems**. That's always been the nuance. The long-running saying you remember is likely a distillation of three things: - The genuine early cheap-electricity pockets. - The enduring marketing of heat pumps, which have been around since the 1950s but got really good by the 2000s. - The desire to frame electricity as the “fuel of the future.” So you're not wrong that the claim has been around forever. But the claim was never a blanket rule for all electric heating in all places. It was conditional then, and it's conditional now. The newer numbers just reflect the current price snapshot, which, for resistance heat, hasn't changed the fundamental pecking order: gas usually wins unless electricity is very cheap or a heat pump is in play. I think where we've been stuck is that you’ve been defending the long-haul wisdom that *electric heating can be the cheaper option overall*, which is true, while I’ve been trying to explain why it didn’t replace gas everywhere—because the simple resistance version usually isn't cheaper month-to-month. Both can be right at the same time, and they don't require you to ignore what you’ve read or heard for years.
